56 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634556 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186634663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634556 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186634689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634556 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186642303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634556 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186642349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634556 #edb/ref 1039 "Converting Between Canonical and Cross Product Form for a Given Straight Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634556 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186634576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634556 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fd88a3e9-cfa1-5b3f-b1c5-6c1939e13dd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634557 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186634581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634557 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186634593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634557 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186634637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634557 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186634679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634557 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186642312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634557 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Cross Product Equation of a Line That Passes Through Two Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634557 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186634647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634557 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fecaf27b-659c-59dd-887f-f154d5f83bd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634699 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186634717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634699 #edb/ref 1061 17893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634699 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "052673c3-9d85-5e36-8e43-85f94f953678" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634699 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186634728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634700 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186634719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634700 #edb/ref 1061 17900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634700 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0cfe4750-b8f2-5f8f-b002-338e2c0d9c20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634700 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186634729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634707 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186634720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634707 #edb/ref 1061 17895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634707 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4d9aae50-2188-5d30-b397-fa50bf19c71b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634707 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186634556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634717 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186634707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634717 #edb/ref 1061 17897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634717 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b56ff46c-db1c-5ceb-a898-e82930114225" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634717 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186634549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634719 #edb/ref 1061 17902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d80b6fc2-ecd5-57be-bc5e-b5525b595a71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186634557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634720 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186634700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634720 #edb/ref 1061 17898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634720 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dba17dcb-861d-58a3-998f-944fccf2581d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634720 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186634552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634725 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186634549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634725 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186634552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634725 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186634556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634725 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186634557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634725 #edb/ref 1031 "Writing the Vector Equation of a Line Using the Cross Product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634725 #edb/ref 1033 1800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634725 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "b42483d3-3ed0-5381-808b-e4606d462d24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634728 #edb/ref 1064 "To write the equation of a line that passes through a point $P$ (with position vector $\\mathbf{p}$) and has a direction vector $\\mathbf{v},$ one option is to use the vector equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r &=p+tv,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there is also another way, which is to write the **equation of a line using the cross product**, as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-  \\mathbf{p} ) \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we have a line that passes through the point $P(4,1,-5)$ and has a direction vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 1,0,-1 \\rangle$ (see the picture below).\n\n![](images/6c/6cd052e948a46524e08b1305a59019b052e71c7b9ce6e5376f49c4cbcc56a737.png)\n\nThe equation of the line can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\langle 4,1,-5 \\rangle) \\times \\langle 1,0,-1 \\rangle = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nTo understand why the formula works, notice that for any point $R,$ this point lies on the line if and only if the vector $\\overrightarrow{PR}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}.$ Now, we know that the cross product of two parallel vectors is always $\\mathbf{0}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PR} \\parallel \\mathbf{v} \n\t\\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad \n\t\\overrightarrow{PR} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, since $\\overrightarrow{PR} = \\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p},$ we get the desired equation\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p}) \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634728 #edb/ref 1065 3247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634728 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "561a4258-eba4-511e-9c5b-75bf4de09801" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634728 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634729 #edb/ref 1064 "If we have an equation of a line in the form $\\left( \\mathbf{r} - \\mathbf{p} \\right) \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we can expand the parentheses, though we must remember that the cross product is non-commutative. This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\times v & =0 \\\\ r\\times v-p\\times v & =0 \\\\ r\\times v & =p\\times v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are both constant vectors, we can denote $\\mathbf{c} = \\mathbf{p} \\times \\mathbf{v}$ to simplify the equation. So, we obtain an alternative form of the equation of the line using the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{c}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634729 #edb/ref 1065 3250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634729 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6d9d8623-8c1b-54a8-8923-62467688f5e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186634729 #edb/ref 1067 "Alternative Form of the Equation of a Line Using the Cross Product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641807 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186642370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641807 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186642371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641807 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186642397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641807 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186642403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641807 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186642408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641807 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641807 #edb/ref 1045 "Writing the Vector Equation of a Line Using the Cross Product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641807 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186642397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641807 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641807 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186634725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641807 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "27418534-46a6-59f7-a2ab-b40d811f93eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641840 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=20i-40j+20k" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641840 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641840 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b4e5162-7c7b-546f-ad92-ac22806bc1ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641876 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=-20i+40j-20k" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186641876 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1